// `extern` INSIDE AN `object`, which both fronts used to answer differently: v3's own parser
// refused it as an unsupported member, and uniml dropped the keyword and gave the member the `???`
// body it gives any bodyless one — so the default front ACCEPTED the program and it died at run
// time with a JVM stack trace, no position and no name. `corpus-report.sh` ranks that CRASH, below
// an honest refusal, and it is invariant I-3: one language, two answers.
//
// NO `.expected`, so this gate requires a REFUSAL CARRYING A POSITION. That is the right verdict
// for this program rather than an accident of it being unfinished: `fsx.exists` is a host function
// no lane implements, and a call to one is refused at the CALL, exactly as a top-level extern is.
// An object extern that a lane DOES implement resolves instead — `resolveExtern` is the same
// function for both, keyed on the qualified name.
object fsx:
  extern def exists(path: String): Bool

def main() = println(fsx.exists("v3").toString)
